1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Pricing is not a personality test. It is scope first, then a number that lets you deliver well and still eat. Fancy consulting frameworks can wait outside.

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:21,141
The order. Never start with the number. Start with what is included, what is excluded, how long it actually takes, and what done looks like. The number comes last.

3
00:00:21,541 --> 00:00:29,319
Step one. Write the outcome in one sentence. What the buyer has when you are finished. Concrete beats impressive every time.

4
00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:41,570
Step two. List the actual work. Be honest about hours. Include the boring parts: revisions, admin, research, waiting on the client. Then multiply by a sustainable hourly floor you can live with.

5
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:53,452
Step three. Add the risk buffer. New client? Unclear brief? Tight deadline? Add twenty to forty percent. Underpricing is not a growth strategy. It is a slow way to burn out.

6
00:00:53,852 --> 00:01:04,222
Step four. Round to a clean number and explain it. Prefer whole hundreds or simple packages. One sentence why this price protects the result for both of you.

7
00:01:04,622 --> 00:01:13,881
Boundaries. What happens when scope creeps. Write it down. Extra requests become a new quote. Niceness without boundaries is just unpaid labor with better manners.

8
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:26,504
Your next move. Price one real service today. Use the four steps. Write the number. Put it on the one-page offer. Send it. Adjust only after real data, not after a panic spiral.

